{
  /* Typechecks the TypeScript under scripts/. Added 2026-09-30 because nothing
     did: `scripts/eval/eval-refute-screen-report.ts` called `needsGemma(r.screen)`
     for months after `needsGemma` gained a required second parameter, so it
     passed `undefined` and threw inside `hasCheckableContent` — crashing
     `pnpm eval:refute-screen` at its reporting stage. tsc would have caught the
     arity on the day the signature changed.

     Options mirror tsconfig.server.json via `extends` (bundler resolution,
     .ts-extension imports, erasableSyntaxOnly — the scripts run under bun) so
     the two cannot drift. Two deliberate additions:

     - "strict": the server project does not set it, but this coverage is new, so
       it starts strict rather than being tightened later against a moving
       target. It is what makes an implicitly-any callback parameter an error.
     - "allowJs" with "checkJs" off: scripts/*.ts import sibling .mjs helpers
       (e.g. scripts/lib/natural-sort.mjs). Letting tsc read those infers real
       types instead of `any`, which is both better than TS7016 and better than a
       hand-written .d.mts that could drift from the JS.

     NOT included: scripts/lib, which carries 62 errors of its own and wants its
     own pass. Its .ts files still enter this program where the trees below
     import them, so nothing they export goes unchecked at the call site. */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.server.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.scripts.tsbuildinfo",
    "strict": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": false
  },
  "include": [
    "scripts/eval/**/*.ts",
    "scripts/aux/**/*.ts",
    "scripts/assess/**/*.ts",
    "scripts/required/**/*.ts"
  ]
}
